Investigate occasional wiggles in outputs.

Left over from the TransportSystem interface work. Everything else in that plan
landed; these are the pieces that were deliberately not done, with the reason.

  - Per-hook vectorisation in the Python trampoline. `override_for("SigmaFn_v")`
    and the other `_v` names are looked up but never inserted into
    method_overrides, so they can only throw: implementing `ComputePhysics` and
    `ComputePhysicsDerivatives` together remains the only vectorised route. A
    case that wants to vectorise one hook and leave the rest pointwise cannot.
    Registering the `_v` names in initializeOverrides is most of the fix; the
    rest is dropping the all-or-nothing classification.

  - The autodiff Source still receives the *stored* sigma, not sigma_hat.
    State::sigmaHat exists and is correct, but AutodiffTransportSystem::Sources
    builds its RealVector from the stored (negated) value, so a case that starts
    using its sigma argument gets the opposite sign from the one SigmaFn
    returned. Flipping it also needs a compensating negation in
    dSources_dsigma, because autodiff would then differentiate with respect to
    sigma_hat while the solver wants d/d(stored sigma).

    The reason this is not already done: *nothing in the tree reads the incoming
    sigma at all*. AdjointPlasma threads it into Sn/Spi/Spe without using it --
    as the C++ MirrorPlasma did before it was removed -- and every
    dSources_dsigma is identically
    zero. So the change is unobservable today and no test would catch getting it
    wrong -- which is exactly why it should not be made blind. Write a physics
    case with a genuinely sigma-dependent source first, check it against a
    closed form, then flip.

  - GlobalState still crosses to Python as a dict of (nPoints, nVars) arrays
    keyed "Variable"/"Derivative"/"Flux"/"Aux"/"Scalars". State became a named
    view; GlobalState did not, because a dict of whole-batch arrays is what the
    vectorised and JAX paths actually want. Worth revisiting only if the key
    strings start causing the same trouble the pointwise ones did.

  - The out-of-tree paths are verified by hand, not by CI. Both were exercised
    end to end -- a one-file C++ case compiled against the installed headers,
    dlopened via PhysicsPlugins and run; and `pip install .` into a fresh venv
    with a Python case run from the user's own directory through the `manta`
    command -- but neither is an automated test. The C++ one needs `make
    install` to a temp prefix and a compiler invocation; the Python one needs a
    venv. Both are slow enough to want their own CI job rather than a place in
    `make test`.


Found since, and left undone deliberately rather than overlooked. Each says what
would have to exist before the fix could be checked, which in every case is the
reason it was not made:

  - dGdaux_Vec still differentiates the wrong functional. It is the last piece
    of the mass-matrix/quadrature-weight confusion that DerivativeSubVector
    carried: GFn reports G = sum_m w_m g(Z_m), so dG/dphi_i is w_i dg/dphi|_i,
    but dGdaux_Vec computes Int dg/dphi phi_i dx with the basis's Gauss rule.
    Same class of error as the one fixed for u, q and sigma -- invisible in every
    aggregate, because the discrepancy annihilates constants, and visible only
    per node.

    Left alone for now because it is not reachable the same way. Spatial adjoint
    parameters are what exposed the u/q/sigma version, and there is no
    per-node aux gradient to compare against finite differences; and unlike the
    three it replaces, dGdaux_Vec is *live* -- dgFn_dphi is the only pointwise dg
    hook a case can still reach, python/Tests/test_adjoint_aux.py overrides it,
    and adjoint_derivative_vectors_match_gauss_quadrature pins the current
    behaviour deliberately.

    Fixing it means either weighting dgFn_dphi at the nodes instead of
    integrating it, or moving aux onto the batched dg hook the way the
    superconvergent branch already has (SystemSolver.cpp:1318 does
    cwiseProduct(b1) on dGdvars.cellwiseAux). The second is tidier and drops a
    hook; the first keeps dgFn_dphi working for cases that override it. Either
    way the test above has to change with it, and something has to be built that
    can actually see the difference -- an nAux > 0 case with spatial parameters
    would do it.

  - PyAdjointProblem::dAux disagrees with the Python dAux it calls. The
    trampoline (PyAdjointProblem.hpp:217) hands the Python method a raw
    GlobalState, but JAXAdjointProblem.dAux expects a converted jax State,
    because the only caller that matters -- ComputePhysicsDerivatives, which is
    what SystemSolver.cpp:1661 invokes -- is @Physics_Decorator and converts
    before calling it. So `AdjointProblem.dAux(...)` from Python raises a vmap
    in_axes error, while the path the solver takes works. dSigma and dSources
    have the same shape and presumably the same split.

    Harmless today: nothing in the tree calls the trampoline. Left alone
    because fixing it means choosing which contract the Python dAux has, and
    the decorated-state one is load-bearing for ComputePhysicsDerivatives --
    so the honest fix is probably to stop binding these three, the way
    ScalarGPrime and ComputePhysicsDerivatives already are not. Found while
    fixing the JAXAuxTest xfail; it cost a wrong turn there, because a test
    driving AdjointProblem.dAux looks like it exercises the solver's path and
    does not.

  - No C++ test asserts that a scalar declared differential has a time
    derivative in its G. Without one, IDACalcIC is handed a row whose every
    quantity it holds fixed, and it fails with IDA_LINESEARCH_FAIL (-13) before
    the first step -- a message about the linesearch, for a defect in the
    declaration. That is what kept python-physics/mirror-plasma's voltage
    controller from ever starting, and the C++ MirrorPlasma carried the same
    misdeclaration before it was removed, so this is not specific to the Python
    surface. The Python side now pins it in
    test_a_differential_scalars_constraint_reaches_a_calcic_unknown; the C++
    equivalent belongs next to checkScalarDerivative in ScalarJacobianTests.cpp
    and is cheap -- call ScalarGPrime and assert dGdot is not identically zero
    for each differential scalar. Two of the three fixtures there would have to
    be checked against it first.

  - AutodiffTransportSystem::dSigmaFn_dp, dSources_dp and MMS_Source
    (PhysicsCases/AutodiffTransportSystem.cpp) still evaluate the plain,
    geometry-blind Flux/Source overloads directly, while every other hook --
    SigmaFn, Sources, AuxG, and every dSigmaFn_.../dSources_.../dAuxG_...
    derivative -- now goes exclusively through the geometry-aware overloads
    added for the field-model coupling. The two sets agree today only because
    the plain overload is still what every case's Flux/Source body actually
    is: nothing yet gives the geometry-aware overload content that differs
    from it. That stops being true the moment a case's real physics genuinely
    depends on geometry, because the plain overload is the mandatory pure
    virtual -- such a case is *forced* to write some body for it just to
    compile, and dSigmaFn_dp/dSources_dp will silently differentiate that
    fictional, geometry-blind stand-in instead of the real flux/source, while
    MMS_Source will manufacture a source against the wrong equation entirely.
    Same failure shape as a missing adjoint block: a perfectly plausible G (or
    a manufactured solution that still converges, at the right rate, to the
    wrong function) with nothing pointing at why.

    Left for Task 10, which owns the adjoint's geometry coupling and is the
    first place a case combining dSigmaFn_dp/dSources_dp (or useMMS) with a
    real geometry dependence would actually appear. The fix in each case is to
    build `geom` from `s.geom()` (dSigmaFn_dp/dSources_dp) or thread a
    nonzero-width geom through the local `State` and every direct Flux/Source
    call (MMS_Source), the way SigmaFn/Sources/every other hook in that file
    already do, and then write the case that would have caught the old
    behaviour being wrong before changing it.


Superconvergence follow-ons (see Tests/README.md for what is already verified):

  - Paper II's HDG(ABC) / HDG+ family: W_h = P_{k+1} with tau ~ 1/h makes u_h
    itself superconverge, with no postprocessing, and reaches k+2 at k = 0 too.
    Needs mixed polynomial degrees across fields, which the single-k assumption in
    DGSoln::Map, every block in Matrices.cpp, AdjointVectors.cpp, the pybind11
    casters and the restart format all rest on. tau ~ 1/h also changes every
    existing run.

  - No manufactured case has more than one aux variable, more than one scalar, or
    both at once. The order studies cover nAux = 1 and nScalars = 1 separately, so
    a block-indexing error that only appears at the second of either would not
    show up in them. SystemSolverMatrixTests.cpp covers the layout with two
    scalars and one aux, and ScalarJacobianTests.cpp with three scalars, but
    neither measures a rate.

  - Spatial adjoint parameters with Superconvergent = true currently throw. They
    index the parameter vector by node, so the star node set would redefine how
    many parameters there are.


The regression suite compares only `u`, never the `q`, `sigma` and `u_star` that
every run also writes. Closing it means regenerating the references and picking
tolerances for three fields a derivative rougher than `u`. Tests/README.md's
"Regression tests" section has the detail and the evidence that it is real slack.


From the origin/optimize-mode review, still unmerged and worth taking:
  - the branch's Objective2.py changes. Two of them: grad_solver_config gains
    "solveAdjoint" = True, and the primal path stops going through
    StellaratorFun and builds the case directly for G and the pressure. Small,
    but objective2.py also does `from stellarator2 import StellaratorTransport`
    hardwired, which is the reason scan_eq_ambipolar.py is not yet coherent:
    it constructs stellarator_multichannel directly *and* calls make_objective,
    which builds the single-channel case. Making the class injectable -- a
    keyword defaulting to the present one -- would fix that without touching the
    four consumers configured the old way, but it is a design change on code
    nothing here can run, so it wants a decision rather than a guess.
  - promoting stellarator_multichannel.py over stellarator2.py, if that is
    wanted. It is a rename plus the st_config of objective2.py,
    desc_optimize.py, scan_eq.py, desc_optimize_bfgs.py and the notebook:
    SourceCenter/SourceHeight/SourceWidth -> HeatSourceCenter/Height/Width.
    Mechanical, but unverifiable here, so it was not done on spec.
  - why the branch removed both `#pragma omp parallel for` from
    TransportSystem.hpp's batched defaults -- ask eatocco; may be a real finding
    about OpenMP against a Python trampoline

  The drivers, stellarator_state.py and the multi-channel rewrite itself are in
  python-physics/stellarator/ now; that README says which of them run against
  what. shard_inputs came with the rewrite.
